{
  "name": "What to Do When Your Basement Floods (and After)",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "It’s a nightmare scenario: you head downstairs and find water in your basement. Before you even think about finishing that space, you absolutely have to address the water problem. Many folks jump right into cosmetic fixes, but trust me, that’s a costly mistake. First, identify the source of the water—is it a leaky pipe, a crack in the foundation, or poor exterior drainage? Once you know, get it fixed permanently. This might mean waterproofing the exterior, sealing cracks, or updating your gutters. Only after the basement is consistently dry should you even consider starting a finishing project. Skipping this crucial step is one of the most common and expensive errors people make. If you're unsure about the source or the fix, it's definitely time to call in a professional who specializes in basement moisture issues. They can properly assess the situation and recommend the right long-term solutions, protecting your investment in a finished basement down the road. Don't let a wet basement ruin your dreams of a functional, beautiful new living space!",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
